WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:11.150
Hey Wisecrack, Michael here. Today, we’re
talking about the human embodiment of the

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.179
awkward pause the king of cringe himself,
Larry David.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:18.440
Hey, edible underwear!

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.080
Between Seinfeld and Curb Your Enthusiasm,
our culture has spent more than thirty years

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.700
laughing at Larry David’s characters and
the uncomfortable situations they find themselves

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.980
in. Whether it’s Elaine calling a baby ugly
in front of its parents.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.309
Some ugly baby huh? What did you say?

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.340
Or Larry returning to a dinner party that
he ruined in order to retrieve his watch.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.359
Well… bye.

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:42.290
We just can’t get enough of this brand of
emotional masochism.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:46.579
But here’s the question: does the pain of
watching Jerry steal an old woman’s rye

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:51.060
bread reveal some greater truth about how
we exist in the world? What unspoken mechanics

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.250
of human interaction does Larry teach us when
a hug goes on just a little too long. Let’s

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.070
find out in this Wisecrack Edition on the
philosophy of Curb Your Enthusiasm. Spoilers

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.500
ahead for a show with more golf than plot.
And some Seinfeld?

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:59.529
Curb Your Enthusiasm is a show about social
interaction or, rather, when social interaction

00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:04.549
fails spectacularly. The show follows a fictionalized
version of Larry David as he wanders around

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:09.020
LA annoying and or infuriating his friends
and family by ignoring social conventions

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.050
that most of us intuitively understand.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.510
“You can’t be sincere about apologizing
and then snack on pistachio nuts!” “What?

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.930
I can eat and apologize.”

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.760
Very little actually happens on Curb. Larry
and his friends will incessantly bicker over

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.250
trivial questions of social propriety,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.381
“You don’t want to be next to me when
you’re dead? I can assume you don’t want

00:02:26.381 --> 00:02:27.739
to be next to me when you’re alive.”

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:32.540
But they rarely make decisions of any real
consequence, Much like Seinfeld before it,

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.540
“It’s about nothing!”

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.910
But aside from giving us cautionary tales
about eating junior mints in an operating

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.290
theater, The Larry David Televised Universe
gives us some deep truths about how we exist

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.610
with our fellow humans. That is, in breaking
the rules, or at least arguing about them,

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.519
David gets at the truth of why social norms
exist in the first place.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:54.060
To understand how - we need to turn to sociologist
Erving Goffman. In his book The Presentation

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.760
of Self in Everyday Life, Goffman argues that
social interaction is a kind of performance

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.120
that we are all putting on for each other,
every minute of every day. Like actors in

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.849
a play, we spend most of our time hiding our
true selves behind constructed identities

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.330
made up of certain expected costumes, behaviors,
and lines of dialogue. Goffman contends that,

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:17.700
not only are we performers, but highly adaptable
ones, able to switch roles in seconds, and

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.290
quickly study up on new ones as needed.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:24.840
In a single day, for instance, one person
might take on the roles of mother, wife, doctor,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.999
Reddit shitposter and more. In the process
said person makes profound, rapid changes

00:03:29.999 --> 00:03:34.019
in their manner, speech patterns, etc., without
even having to think about it. Or, if you’re

00:03:34.019 --> 00:03:38.880
George Costanza, you might vacillate between
unemployed curmudgeon, screenwriter, pretend

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.180
marine biologist, corporate sycophant, and
man who would like to be lathered in oil

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:48.450
According to Goffman, when we encounter others,
we refer back to previous experiences to quickly

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.739
decide what sort of scene we will act out
together: waiter/patron, salesperson/customer,

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:58.230
host/guest, etc. In a given interaction, the
person with less at stake will tend to offer

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:02.750
the other a bit of leeway in setting the scene,
with the expectation that the same kindness

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.889
will be extended back their way in the future.
In this way, “Together the participants

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.700
contribute to a single overall definition
of the situation... a ’working consensus’.”

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.690
In other words, a big part of being human
is working with other humans to select and

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:21.080
act out social scripts appropriate to a given
moment. Sometimes these patterns of interaction

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:25.699
are so well established that they become almost
unconscious. Most of us have internalized

00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:28.530
that you don’t talk in theaters ,double-dip
at parties

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:33.370
Did you just double dip that chip? Excuse
me? You double dipped the chip!

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.540
Or break the social contract implied in picking
someone up from the airport. Most people also

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.160
understand what an appropriate hug duration
is.. On the other hand, sometimes people run

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.860
into completely new situations—or hit snags
in more conventional ones—and have to work

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.530
together to define that situation and react
accordingly. Or, you know, they try to do

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:57.130
that and fail completely: Like when Larry
becomes accidentally aroused after a hug goes

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.830
on for longer than expected. You know, she
went over the appropriate amount of time that

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:07.590
I can have human contact without getting aroused.I
only have five seconds. After that, it's out

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.759
of my control]. Larry accordingly has to deal
with the fallout from his friends. You hug

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:15.780
my auntie, man, you stab her in the stomach?
- Oh well - What the fuck was that? She hugged

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.480
me and she held that hug for over five seconds.]
What th- I have no control after five seconds!

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.970
Larry finds himself in another script-breaking
situation, in “Kamikaze Bingo,” when his

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.389
pharmacist offers a negative opinion of a
drug prescribed to Larry’s father.

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:33.650
You know there is another drug out on the
market that I personally like a lot better.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.030
Most of us might assume, incorrectly, that
the job of a pharmacist is to just shut up,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.220
listen to a doctor, and give us our Klonopin.
But in this case, our guy bucks the assumed

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:47.520
protocol for pharmacist/customer interaction--and
forces Larry to choose whose performance to

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.520
trust:

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:56.319
I’m a doctor Larry well he’s a pharmacist.
Yes and I’m a doctor. He’s a pharmacist.

00:05:56.319 --> 00:06:01.530
In all these cases, the participants are constantly
making choices, for better or for worse,about

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.810
how to perform, and how to respond to the
performance of others.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:09.690
In a smooth social interaction, all the various
definitions of the situation will quickly

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:14.199
line up, and “working consensus” will
be achieved. Just as many of us have internalized

00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:19.259
“normal” behavior, we have also been heavily
socialized to tolerate the behaviors of others,

00:06:19.259 --> 00:06:23.199
even when it’s obviously fake. Goffman contends
that this willingness to be flexible with

00:06:23.199 --> 00:06:27.949
other people —to take their performances
at face value—is what allows societies of

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:32.409
all sizes to function. Everything from your
dinner party to the economy depends on all

00:06:32.409 --> 00:06:35.180
of us cutting each other a certain amount
of slack.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:40.229
Enter Larry, whose entire schtick revolves
around his inability--or his outright refusal--to

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:42.490
reach a working consensus with anyone.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:47.660
While it’s impossible to reduce Curb Your
Enthusiasm to a formula, one of the show’s

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.669
most-used gags is simply to put Larry in a
social situation and have him vehemently disagree

00:06:52.669 --> 00:06:56.951
with someone else’s definition of it. Again
in “Kamikaze Bingo,” for instance, when

00:06:56.951 --> 00:07:02.240
Larry learns that his friend Yoshi’s father
survived his tour of duty as a WWII kamikaze

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.449
pilot, he insists that

00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:10.260
kamikaze pilot kind of implies that, you know
a kamikaze pilot is a pilot who crashed and

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.260
died, right?”

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:15.250
He refuses to drop the point and find a working
consensus with his wife and friend, even though

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:19.930
his questions make Yoshi visibly uncomfortable.
Even when it becomes clear that his prodding

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.770
eventually led Yoshi to attempt suicide, Larry
still refuses to concede:

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:29.550
“There’s a lot of kamikaze pilots that
are still alive.” “You say that. And I

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:34.849
say to you that they are not kamikaze pilots!”
Larry is completely inflexible, rejecting

00:07:34.849 --> 00:07:39.389
anyone else’s definition of a situation.
He derails social gatherings by obsessing

00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:44.610
over minor annoyances that most adults would
ignore for the sake of a quiet meal with friends.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.060
You’re really the only person who has a
problem with the water. Yeah it’s just water,

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:54.630
what are you so sensitive about? I want you
to go. I'm getting kicked out?

00:07:54.630 --> 00:08:00.509
When family friend Cliff Cobb tells an anecdote
about his grandfather inventing the Cobb salad,

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:01.509
Larry thinks he’s lying.

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:02.509
It’s my grandfather’s salad.

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:06.520
Rather than letting it go like a sane person,
he commissions his secretary to do a research

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.580
project on the origins of the Cobb salad,

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:14.240
Here’s what I want you to do. Ok. I want
you to go on the internet and get some information

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.509
for me on the Cobb salad. Later confronting
Cliff with the new information:

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:28.289
“The Cobb salad, my friend, was invented
in 1937, by Bob Cobb, at the Brown Derby!”

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:32.849
When Larry is in the room, it is almost impossible
to come to a single definition of the situation

00:08:32.849 --> 00:08:36.030
that will allow the group to continue talking
in a productive way.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:41.920
The way Larry constantly sabotages any working
consensus is the key to the show’s famous

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.230
cringiness. As Goffman points out, “When
these disruptive events occur... the participants

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.330
find themselves lodged in an interaction for
which the situation has been wrongly defined

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:55.260
and is now no longer defined. All the participants
may come to feel ill at ease.”

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.450
Curb Your Enthusiasm offers viewers the social
equivalent of the physical humor found in

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:04.260
Tom and Jerry, The Three Stooges, and Jackass
--that is, a chance to laugh at people in

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.290
pain. While Mo and Larry might smack Curley
in the head or grab his nose with a pair of

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:13.460
scissors, our Larry subjects his wife, manager,
and other acquaintances to that awful, prickly

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:17.610
emotional discomfort we feel when a social
encounter is spiraling out of control.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:18.610
When you walk through my door you play by
my rules! You take off your fucking shoes!

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:22.920
You and your little soccer shoes in my house!
My feet have a tendency to get a little chilly

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:35.760
when I take my- Gil get the coats! The coats
are probably a good idea

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:41.120
Larry’s refusal to adapt to any working
consensus outside his narrow worldview causes

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.120
plenty of chaos and the occasional screaming
meltdown. --

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.850
Now I’m saying thank you for helping me,
I’m going to have some cake. No no but you

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.670
can’t change it that’s why you say no
matter what this is, this is the what, that’s

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:55.250
why you ask me and not these other people
because you knew I wouldn’t let you. Larry!

00:09:55.250 --> 00:10:03.710
You told me not to let her have it! She said
no matter what…

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:07.520
We see this play out in “Trick or Treat,”
the season 2 episode that confronts Larry

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.400
with two teenagers pretending to be little
kids on Halloween. Where most adults would

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.830
roll their eyes and toss the girls a few Hershey
bars to make them go away, Larry decides that

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.650
this is the time to make a stand:

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:19.650
Are you kidding? It’s halloween?

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.150
“Yeah it doesn’t mean that you’re entitled
to go around to people’s homes and bilk

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.150
them out of candy.”

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.940
When he calls the cops to report the resultant
vandalism of his house, the officer claims

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.450
that Larry started the whole mess by refusing
to give the teens any candy:

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.650
[“There’s kind of a social contract that
you enter when you open that door. They say

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:39.390
‘Trick or Treat,’ I would advise you,
give the treat.”

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.880
Larry’s wife Cheryl points directly to the
big gap between Larry’s definition of the

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.370
situation and the working consensus that gets
the rest of us through Halloween without getting

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.140
the words “Bald Asshole” spray-painted
on our houses.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:55.220
In later seasons, Larry is even called upon
to use his unique capacity for tactlessness

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.500
to solve his friends’ problems. When an
attempt to get a mutual friend’s wife to

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.800
stop saying “LOL” backfires, Larry’s
manager, Jeff, finally puts a label on his

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:08.520
unwillingness to bend to social norms:
You know what you are? You’re a social assassin.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:09.520
Jesus.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:13.370
When the social assassin makes a “hit,”
he is killing a working consensus that is

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.960
disagreeable to another member of the group—one
who is too afraid to put forward their own

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:22.640
alternate definition of a situation. Because
Larry would rather be right than be part of

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.570
a functioning social group, he will call out
any aspect of a performance that he doesn’t

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:28.570
like.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:31.930
But how, specifically, do people create these
performances for each other? And how does

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.640
Larry disrupt them?

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:38.200
One way is by ridiculing and playing around
with what Goffman referred to as the individual’s

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:42.940
‘front,’ or “the items that we most
intimately identify with the performer...like

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:47.690
insignia of office or rank; clothing; speech
patterns; facial expressions; bodily gestures;

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:48.690
and the like.”

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:53.670
In Larry work, characters routinely fixate
on one or more items in another character’s

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:57.700
personal front. Pieces of clothing--and what
they supposedly reveal about the people that

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:02.320
wear them--are key story elements in multiple
Seinfeld episodes. Consider Jerry’s horror

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.080
at the thought of having his front tarnished
by the pirate shirt.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:07.000
But I don’t want to be a pirate!

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.080
Or Jerry and Elaine fixating on how to interpret
the meaning of a cape,

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:14.800
Is he wearing a cape? I believe he is wearing
a cape.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:17.960
Or Jerry’s dismay over the cowboy boots
he’s forced to wear:

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:20.140
I don’t want to be a cowboy!

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:24.580
These characters spend an inordinate amount
of time worrying about other people’s fronts,

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:28.270
and trying to preserve their own You know
the message you’re sending out with those

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:33.820
sweatpants? You’re telling the world, I
give up, I can’t compete in a normal society,

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:38.750
I’m miserable so I might as well be comfortable].
This carries over into Curb Your Enthusiasm.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:43.040
Larry is often preoccupied with the minutiae
of other peoples’ “fronts.” Someone’s

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.790
choice in headgear, for instance, will affect
whether he decides to help them pick up a

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:48.340
dropped golf ball:

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:54.990
“Same hat, no string, no question I pick
it up. I I don’t kn- I see certain items

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.570
and I recoil.”

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.830
Or whether he’ll take a friend into a restaurant:

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:02.500
Proud Jews wear yarmulkes. Yeah, be proud
here in the parking lot. Don’t be proud

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:03.500
in there.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:08.820
At one point, Larry even picks a fight with
Ted Danson--at the man’s own birthday party--for

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.780
having his catering service wear bow ties:

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:14.090
It looks elegant. I feel like you are putting
on airs or something.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:17.610
But the show’s obsession with front comes
through most clearly in one of the subplots

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.370
to “Trick or Treat,” in which Larry starts
wearing golf clothes on days when he isn’t

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:25.660
actually playing golf. This confuses his friends,
who don’t see the point of adopting a front

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:29.430
that communicates “golf” if one has no
intention of hitting the links:

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.770
Larry simply likes the way he looks in a tucked-in
polo shirt and slacks

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:38.390
I like the outfit, I like the way it looks.
According to his definition of the situation,

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.750
wearing golf clothes does not necessarily
imply that one is going to or coming from

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:43.750
a game.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.790
Larry, no one would wear a shirt like that
unless they were going to golf.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:49.810
Despite his preoccupation with the front of
others, he decides that he can add these items

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:53.460
to his own front without making a statement
about what he’s doing for the afternoon.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:58.190
But for the other characters, Larry’s front
is so tied up with the practice of golfing

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:02.430
that they refuse to believe him when he explains
he doesn’t have a round scheduled that day:

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:06.690
You aid you weren't golfing! Why the hell
are you wearing those clothes! Because I like

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:07.690
the way they look!

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:11.980
All of that notwithstanding, Larry isn’t
always on the outside looking in when it comes

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:16.630
to deciding what’s appropriate and what
isn’t. On rare occasions, he is temporarily

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:21.040
able to win others over to his side, in ways
that line up with Goffman’s description

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.580
of group, or “team,” performances.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:28.360
According to Goffman, a group of people defining
a situation together operates a little differently

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:32.910
from an individual trying to do the same.
An individual can create a complex definition

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:37.990
of situation for themself. In contrast, a
team working together to define a situation

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:42.780
will often have to boil things down to a “thin
party line” --the lowest common denominator

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.350
that everyone involved can agree on. Groups,
in other words, tend to define situations

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:51.730
in broad strokes—rather than developing
nuanced interpretations to events, they will

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:56.140
judge things as simply “good” or “bad,”
“acceptable” or “unacceptable,” etc.

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:01.440
The members of the team may feel compelled
to defend the party line, even if it is simplistic.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:06.110
We see this play out on a micro level in “Kamikaze
Bingo.” When the host’s brother in law’s

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:10.160
suicide attempt interrupts poker night, those
remaining around the card table aren’t sure

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:15.140
what to do next--there is no ready definition
for their situation. Larry is the first to

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:16.630
begin making one up:

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:25.440
“Well, you know. We’re here.” That’s
true cause ya know I had to really finagle

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.920
to get out tonight I’m kind of happy to
be anywhere. Ya know we can’t leave the

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:33.800
house, ya know it’s, they’re gone. We
don’t have keys. We don’t know the alarm

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:40.520
code or anything. And a pizza is coming. And
a pizza coming. Ya we got a pizza coming.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:45.280
Not wanting to miss out on a night of poker,
the boys work together to develop the transparently

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:49.700
stupid party line of “a pizza is coming.”
They fall back on it when the host returns

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:52.880
from the hospital and calls them out on their
inconsiderate behavior:

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:55.750
You know pizza was coming, it would’ve been
rude to leave a pizza coming. It would have

00:15:55.750 --> 00:16:00.170
been rude to stop and pick it up on your way
home too. We didn’t know your alarm code

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:05.030
But Curb Your Enthusiasm also explores this
dynamic on a much larger scale. One of the

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:09.890
show’s recurring themes is Larry’s relationship
to Judaism--or, rather, his lack thereof.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:14.980
Curb portrays segments of the American Jewish
community as a Goffman-style team: a collection

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:19.200
of individuals working overtime to establish
what counts as “correct” behavior for

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:23.800
the group, and condemning anything that doesn’t
fall within those boundaries. As always, Larry

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:27.470
has a tough time achieving working consensus
with the group, which results in him being

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:30.980
labelled as not good enough in his performance
of the faith:

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:32.100
You’re a lousy Jew!

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:36.240
Larry takes on this mantle with glee--nothing
seems to make him happier than being yelled

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:41.320
at by someone who is trying to shame him for
disagreeing with their definition of the situation,

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.810
i.e. what it means to be Jewish.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:46.460
In “Trick or Treat,” Larry gets into a
feud with a man who hears him humming a Wagner

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:47.460
tune to his wife Cheryl:

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:52.920
I want to know what a Jew is whistling Wagner
for when he was one of the great anti-Semites

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:57.170
of the world. You know what you are? You are
a self-loathing Jew. Am I? Yes yes. LEt me

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:02.440
tell you, I do hate myself but it has nothing
to do with being Jewish!

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:07.140
Of course, the man’s claim that good Jewish
people should not enjoy Wagner fails to make

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:17.509
an impact on Larry, who rejects the consensus
he represents:

00:17:17.509 --> 00:17:21.990
And in the season 8 episode “Palestinian
Chicken,” Larry’s love for a new Palestinian

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.740
restaurant puts him in conflict with his Jewish
acquaintances.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:29.929
Larry causes friction early on by violating
Marty Funkhauser’s proud-Jewish-man front

00:17:29.929 --> 00:17:31.330
near the entrance to Al-Abbas:

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.879
Don’t you reach for that. Don’t you ever
touch that.

00:17:34.879 --> 00:17:39.080
When Marty later tries to shame Larry for
starting a fling with the Palestinian restaurant

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:41.360
owner,
Larry fires back by attacking his front once

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:44.860
again, making fun of him for taking his religion
and culture so seriously

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:50.370
You’re not even a man anymore! Mommy rabbi
says don’t play, little boy!

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.470
The episode ends with Larry literally caught
between a crowd of Palestinian protestors

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:58.590
on one side of him and a crowd of Jewish protesters
on the other. He’s caught between the situation

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.909
he wants, to eat delicious chicken, and the
one he’s saddled with--a working consensus

00:18:02.909 --> 00:18:05.270
that labels him a religious traitor.

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:14.840
“I have a sister! You, me, and Yasmeen.
The three of us. That’s enough, just get

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:15.840
the fuck over here, just get the fuck over
here. Stop being an idiot. Larry come over

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:16.840
here. Larry! Don’t be a dick.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:20.929
Like many comic figures, Larry gets laughs
by flaunting social norms--but few characters

00:18:20.929 --> 00:18:24.139
are so single-minded in their devotion to
causing friction:

00:18:24.139 --> 00:18:27.380
I will not be intimidated. Even on Halloween.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:31.450
While Curb Your Enthusiasm may make some people
cringe rather than laugh, it would be hard

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:35.929
to find a human being who can watch the show
and be completely unaffected. By directly

00:18:35.929 --> 00:18:40.600
attacking the system that allows us to interact
with our fellow humans, Larry toys with some

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:45.030
of our deepest fears around social failure,
shame, and flat-out awkwardness. Depending

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.789
on your tastes, this is either hilarious--or
terrible.

00:18:47.789 --> 00:18:51.940
It’s cute, right? No. Not really.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:55.450
What Larry moments have you survived in your
own life? Have a favorite episode that we

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:59.600
missed? Let us know in the comments, and as
always, be sure to subscribe and ring that

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:04.840
bell. Thanks to our amazing patrons for supporting
